<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The Compute family's charts against the REAL CDI and KubeVirt — installed onto a fresh k3s by
      charts/bundle/install.sh before the one cluster-backed class here runs.

      ⚠ THE SECOND TEST PROJECT UNDER src/Providers THAT INSTALLS A BUNDLE COMPONENT, AND THE FIRST
      TO INSTALL A `manifest:` ROW. KubeVirtOnAnEmptyCluster runs install.sh, selecting by component, for
      openebs-localpv, containerized-data-importer and kubevirt, imports an image through
      charts/managed/image, provisions a disk through charts/managed/disk, applies
      charts/managed/virtual-machine against KubeVirt's real webhooks and reads the guest Running
      under KVM. CyberCloud.Providers.Compute.Cluster.Conformance, the family's other k3s lane, applies
      the same renders against a derived CRD stub and records that no operator admitted them; this
      project is where an operator does.

      ⚠ ONE CLASS, EIGHT MINUTES, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A PROJECT OF ITS OWN IN THE CLUSTER LANE.
      Measured at 7 m 03 s to 8 m 26 s on a ten-CPU host, most of it install.sh pulling the two
      operators' images. The class landed in test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance and the review
      of #28 asked what that costs on a PR: gate.yml's `test` job runs every cluster-backed suite one
      at a time (build/Build.Test.cs § ClusterBackedSuiteDegree), that chain took 26 m 16 s on the
      runner on 2026-09-15 — read off the job's own log, against a 25-minute budget pr.yml enforces
      and a 30-minute job timeout — and this class would have put it past the timeout on every PR.
      #25 had already split the same chain off the PR for the same measurement: the Cluster lane of
      Test (build/Build.Test.cs § TestLane) runs the *.Cluster.Conformance suites on every merge and in
      nightly.yml's full run, never on a PR. So this project takes that suffix and rides that lane,
      the way CyberCloud.Providers.RecoveryServices.Cnpg.Cluster.Conformance does for the operator it
      installs; the cost is main.yml's `test-cluster` job's, which has a 45-minute timeout and no
      PR budget, and the class remarks carry the readings.

      ⚠ NOT `.Conformance`, AND NOT A CLASS IN THE FAMILY'S `.Cluster.Conformance`. The first would
      put this assembly on every PR, where Test's Fast lane and the Labels gate run without a
      daemon and read "Zero tests ran" as a failure. The second already exists, and its harness
      derives an open-schema stub for every kind its k3s does not serve; the class here asserts first
      that its k3s serves neither kubevirt.io nor cdi.kubevirt.io and then hands install.sh the real
      definitions. Whichever ran first in one process would leave the other a cluster it was not
      written for — a stub install.sh does not own, or a real webhook behind a suite that records
      there is none — the Cnpg project's reason for its own process, and the same boundary here: one
      k3s per process, ClusterSlot.

      ⚠ WHAT MOVED AND WHAT STAYED. The cert-manager, openebs-localpv and cloudnative-pg classes stay in
      test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance: together they cost under five minutes and are the
      install mechanism's own proof. What moved is the one class whose cost is the operators it
      installs rather than the script that installs them. EmptyClusterFixture and BundleInstaller
      stay where they were and are referenced from here, so this project starts its k3s the same way
      and takes the same ClusterSlot permit as that sibling.

      ⚠ THE DAEMON-FREE COMPANION MOVED WITH IT. KubeVirtComponentInstaller reads the installer's dry
      run and needs no cluster; without it a run of this assembly on a machine with no Docker daemon
      would skip its only test and report "Zero tests ran", which the minimum-expected-tests switch
      fails. The same shape CertManagerComponentInstaller gives the bundle suite.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.K3s"/>
        <!-- The raw client, for the half of every assertion here that is deliberately NOT our code. -->
        <PackageReference Include="KubernetesClient"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          For EmptyClusterFixture and BundleInstaller — the fixture that starts one k3s per class under
          ClusterSlot, and the runner of install.sh. Both are that project's and are not copied here,
          for the reason its own csproj gives about ClusterInfrastructure: a second spelling of a
          lock-file name is how two suites come to hold two clusters.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../../test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance.csproj"/>
        <!--
          ⚠ THE FAMILY'S .Contracts, FOR TWO READINGS. KubeVirtOnAnEmptyCluster hands the
          VirtualMachine a real KubeVirt wrote to VirtualMachines.ReadinessOf and VirtualMachines.Matches,
          so the sentence the reconciler would put in a tenant's InProgress message and the comparison
          that decides Converged are both asserted against an object the mutating webhook has been
          through. No reconciler runs here; the objects are applied by this test's own kubectl from the
          charts, exactly as CloudNativePgOnAnEmptyCluster applies its Cluster.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.Compute.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.Compute.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
